    "text": "\n\nFig. 7. Fixed-wing simulation environment. \n\nenvironments from the training distribution to evaluate our approach. Our approach outperformed all baselines and never violated the constraints (Fig. 5) \n\n# _D. Fixed-wing UAV_ \n\nWe repeat our approach with stochastic fixed-wing UAV dynamics to demonstrate that it can extend to highdimensional systems (Fig. 7). We utilize the formulation of the fixed-wing described in [55] with 2nd order RungeKutta (RK2) integration. The state of the system is **x** = [ **r** _,_ **q** _,_ **_δ_** _,_ **v** _,_ **_ω_** _, p_ ] where **r** _∈_ R<sup>3</sup> is the position, **q** _∈_ Q is the orientation, **_δ_** _∈_ R<sup>3</sup> are control surface deflections, **v** _∈_ R<sup>3</sup> is the linear velocity, **_ω_** _∈_ R<sup>3</sup> is the angular velocity, and _p_ is the propeller speed. The control signal is the rate of change of the control surface deflections and of the propeller speed: **u** = [ _ua, ue, ur, up_ ]. We place Gaussian noise over the body frame accelerations, with means of [0 _._ 10 _, −_ 0 _._ 90 _, −_ 0 _._ 89] _s_<sup>_<u>m</u>_2, [1</sup><sup>_._18</sup><sup>_, −_0</sup><sup>_._16</sup><sup>_,_1</sup><sup>_._71]</sup><sup>_<u>rad</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>2and variances</sup> of _diag_ ([1 _._ 71 _,_ 1 _._ 63 _,_ 1 _._ 41]), _diag_ ([10 _._ 05 _,_ 5 _._ 57 _,_ 15 _._ 23]), which was fit from data collected on hardware. We simulated the fixed-wing with a 64 beam 360<sup>_◦_</sup> LiDAR attached on a gimbal such that it remains parallel to the xy-plane. \n\nWe use a quadratic state cost _q_ ( **x** _,_ **u** ) = ( **r** _−_ **x** _G_ )<sup>_T_</sup> _Q_ **r** ( **r** _−_ **x** _G_ ) + **_δ_**<sup>_T_</sup> _Q_ **_δδ_** + **_ω_**<sup>_T_</sup> _Q_ **_ωω_** where _Q_ **r** = _Q_ **_ω_** = _diag_ ([0 _._ 01 _,_ 0 _._ 01 _,_ 0 _._ 01]) and _Q_ **_δ_** = _diag_ ([0 _._ 1 _,_ 0 _._ 1 _,_ 0 _._ 1]). We constrained the altitude to [0 _,_ 5] _m_ , the speed along the _x, y_ plane to _≤_ 8<sup>_<u>m</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>,andtherollrateto[</sup><sup>_−_10</sup><sup>_,_10]</sup><sup>_<u>rad</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>.We</sup> set _L_ = 1, _γ_ = 3, and sampled cluttered environments with a maximum of 20 obstacles. The actor and critic networks trained in 3 million steps over 4.5 hours. In these experiments, we do not count velocity constraint violations in Figures 8, 9. Our approach outperformed both the actor policy and PAC-NMPC with a quadratic terminal cost of _qf_ ( **x** _,_ **u** ) = 10 _._ 0 _· q_ ( **x** _,_ **u** ) (Fig. 8). \n\nTo demonstrate that a value function trained on simple dynamics can aid in controlling more complex systems, we ran experiments utilizing the bicycle value function to control the fixed-wing. We trained the bicycle value function in these environments with _vmin_ = 2, _vmax_ = 8, and no Gaussian noise. Then, we mapped the fixed-wing state to the bicycle state by extracting _px_ , _py_ , _θ_ , _v_ from **r** , **q** , **v** and setting _δ_ = atan(<sup>_<u>L</u>_</sup> _v_<sup>_θ_˙). For the terminal cost, we applied the bicycle value</sup> function plus _q_ ( **x** _,_ **u** ) where _Q_ **r** = _diag_ ([0 _._ 00 _,_ 0 _._ 00 _,_ 0 _._ 1]), \n\n\n\nFig. 8. Fixed-wing environments. Percentage of trials in which the system reached the goal or violated constraints. \n\n\n\nFig. 9. Fixed-wing environments with additional noise. Percentage of trials in which the system reached the goal or violated constraints. \n\n_Q_ **_ω_** = _diag_ ([0 _._ 1 _,_ 0 _._ 1 _,_ 0 _._ 1]) and _Q_ **_δ_** = _diag_ ([1 _,_ 1 _,_ 1]). This outperformed all other approaches, including PAC-NMPC with the fixed-wing value function (Fig. 8). We hypothesize that this may be because the bicycle value function is easier to learn due to its smaller state space. \n\nTo demonstrate that our approach is more resilient when the dynamics uncertainty does not match the training environment, we shifted the mean of the noise over the body accelerations to [3 _._ 10 _,_ 2 _._ 10 _,_ 2 _._ 11] _s_<sup>_<u>m</u>_2and [4</sup><sup>_._18</sup><sup>_,_2</sup><sup>_._84</sup><sup>_,_4</sup><sup>_._71]</sup><sup>_<u>rad</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>2</sup> and repeated the experiments. Our approach was able to maintain higher success rates with both the fixed-wing value function and the bicycle value function than PAC-NMPC with a quadratic terminal cost or the actor policy (Fig. 9). \n\n# VI. HARDWARE EXPERIMENTS \n\nTo evaluate our approach on physical hardware, we used a 1/10<sup>th</sup> scale Traxxas Rally Car platform with a Velodyne Puck LITE LiDAR. We modeled the rally car dynamics as a stochastic bicycle model with Gaussian process noise fit from data. We used the same value function that was trained entirely in the cluttered environment simulation. We also trained a value function with an incorrect wheelbase, _L_ = 0 _._ 5, to demonstrate the utility of our approach even in the presence of model mismatch. \n\nWe generated 20 random environments consisting of a maximum of 8 circular obstacles in an 8 meter by 6 meter space. We discarded environments in which obstacles overlapped or in which no obstacles were blocking the path to \n\n"
